WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.140
If an adult brain made a new neuron, how would you ever know it was new?

2
00:00:08.133 --> 00:00:11.533
The old rule: the neurons you are born with are the ones you keep.

3
00:00:12.517 --> 00:00:14.957
A cell copies its DNA before it divides.

4
00:00:15.937 --> 00:00:18.957
A finished neuron normally does not copy its DNA again.

5
00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:23.570
So give an animal tagged thymidine, a DNA building block.

6
00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:26.290
Cells copying their DNA build it in.

7
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:31.520
A labelled cell can divide again, and each daughter carries about half the label.

8
00:00:32.533 --> 00:00:34.113
So the label dates the DNA.

9
00:00:35.093 --> 00:00:38.713
A strongly labelled neuron was most likely born that day, or later.

10
00:00:39.783 --> 00:00:44.323
DNA repair, or a dying cell copying its DNA, can add label too.

11
00:00:45.483 --> 00:00:46.483
Studies need controls.

12
00:00:47.333 --> 00:00:52.733
In the 1960s, Joseph Altman found labelled cells in the dentate gyrus of rats.

13
00:00:53.783 --> 00:00:55.003
Pause and predict.

14
00:00:55.903 --> 00:00:57.123
Is the labelled cell a neuron?

15
00:01:03.617 --> 00:01:05.337
The light microscope could not tell.

16
00:01:06.197 --> 00:01:09.357
The grains might belong to a glial cell pressed against a neuron.

17
00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:12.470
A birth record, but no identity.

18
00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:15.390
The claims were largely set aside for years.

19
00:01:19.833 --> 00:01:26.153
In 1977, electron microscopy showed that labelled cells in adult rats were neurons.

20
00:01:27.233 --> 00:01:31.873
Then came BrdU, a thymidine look-alike that an antibody can detect.

21
00:01:32.883 --> 00:01:38.083
One cell can now be stained twice, for BrdU and for the neuron protein NeuN.

22
00:01:42.467 --> 00:01:44.187
That is the rule for this lesson.

23
00:01:45.127 --> 00:01:48.267
A neuron counts as new only when the same cells carry two facts.

24
00:01:49.317 --> 00:01:54.097
A birth record, showing their DNA was copied at a known time, so they were most

25
00:01:54.097 --> 00:01:55.537
likely born then or later.

26
00:01:56.437 --> 00:01:57.557
And a neuron identity.

27
00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:03.350
A young-neuron marker, such as doublecortin, tells you a cell's state, not when it

28
00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:04.350
was born.

29
00:02:04.783 --> 00:02:06.983
Takeaway: two facts in the same cells.
